tel 1.0

base corpuscular
import soundness

info   the CRC-32, CRC-64 and Adler-32 checksums, and the incremental `Digestion` every hash is fed through
detail
    _Corpuscular_ holds the three non-cryptographic checksums the compression and container
    formats need — `Crc32` (gzip, PNG, ZIP, XZ), `Crc64` (the ECMA-182 form XZ uses) and
    `Adler32` (zlib) — and the `Digestion` interface through which every hash in Soundness,
    cryptographic or not, is computed incrementally.

    Each checksum is named twice. As a **type**, `Crc32` is an `Algorithm`, a marker which
    names the checksum in a type position, so a value is checksummed with the same
    `digest[Crc32]` as it is hashed with `digest[Sha2[256]]`, through gastronomy. As an
    **object**, `Crc32` makes a running `Crc32.Accumulator`, a mutable `Digestion` for code
    which feeds bytes as they stream past and reads the checksum as the integer a container
    format writes into its trailer.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import alphabets.hexLowerCase
    import cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes
    import providers.javaBaseProvider

    t"123456789".digest[Crc32].serialize[Hex]   // t"cbf43926"

    val crc = Crc32()
    crc.append(t"1234".in[Data])
    crc.append(t"56789".in[Data])
    crc.value                                   // 0xcbf43926L
    ```

    A checksum detects accidental corruption and defends against nothing, so `digest` with
    one is gated behind gastronomy's permit `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`,
    which concedes that no adversary is in the threat model. The JDK's provider,
    `providers.javaBaseProvider`, offers CRC-32 and Adler-32; CRC-64 has no JDK
    implementation, and is offered only by `providers.soundnessProvider`, which uses this
    library's implementations of all three. The same input gives the published check value
    under each:

    ```scala fuel
    alternative CRC-32
      code
          locally:
            import providers.soundnessProvider
            t"123456789".digest[Crc32].serialize[Hex]     // t"cbf43926"
    alternative Adler-32
      code
          locally:
            import providers.soundnessProvider
            t"123456789".digest[Adler32].serialize[Hex]   // t"091e01de"
    alternative CRC-64
      code
          locally:
            import providers.soundnessProvider
            t"123456789".digest[Crc64].serialize[Hex]     // t"995dc9bbdf1939fa"
    ##
    ```

    The library has one source set, `corpuscular.core`, written in portable Scala, so the
    checksums compute the same values on the JVM, Scala.js, Scala Native and WebAssembly.
    `Adler32`, `Algorithm`, `Crc32`, `Crc64` and `Digestion` are exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella.

refer corpuscular.Crc32
refer corpuscular.Crc64
refer corpuscular.Adler32
refer corpuscular.Digestion
refer gastronomy.Digest

entry .Algorithm   the marker type of a hash or checksum algorithm, named in a type position as `digest[Crc32]`
    A trait with no members. Every algorithm Soundness can digest with is a type extending it:
    the checksums `Crc32`, `Crc64` and `Adler32` here, and gastronomy's `Sha2[256]`, `Sha1`,
    `Md5`, `Blake3` and `Murmur3`. Algorithms are never instantiated; a `Digest in Crc32`
    carries its algorithm in its type, so digests from different algorithms are never
    confused.

entry .Digestion   an incremental hash or checksum computation: append bytes, then read the digest
  detail
      A `Digestion` is the mutable state of one hash in progress. It is honestly mutable — a
      `caps.Mutable`, whose `append` and `digest` are `update` methods — so a fresh one is an
      exclusive `Digestion^`, and each computation needs its own. The checksums' accumulators
      (`Crc32.Accumulator`, `Crc64.Accumulator`, `Adler32.Accumulator`) implement it directly,
      and gastronomy's `Hashing` providers supply one for each cryptographic hash.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val digestion: Digestion^ = Crc32()
      digestion.append(t"1234".in[Data])
      digestion.append(t"56789".in[Data])
      digestion.digest()   // the four big-endian bytes cb f4 39 26
      ```

      A streaming consumer which reads into a reusable window feeds it with the windowed
      `#append` rather than snapshotting it: hashing consumes its input synchronously and never
      retains it.
  refer corpuscular.Algorithm
  entry #append   feeds bytes to the computation: all of a `Data`, or a window of a mutable array
      `append(bytes)` hashes the whole of an immutable `Data`. `append(array, start, count)`
      hashes `count` bytes of a mutable array from `start`, which needs only read access to
      it; the default implementation copies the window, and the checksums' accumulators
      override it to read the array in place, with no allocation.
  entry #digest   the result so far, as bytes
      For the checksums, the checksum's value as big-endian bytes: four for `Crc32` and
      `Adler32`, eight for `Crc64`. Whether further appends may follow depends on the
      implementation; the checksums' accumulators allow it, and the result then covers every
      byte appended.

entry .Crc32   the standard CRC-32 checksum, as used by gzip, PNG, ZIP and XZ
  detail
      The reflected CRC-32 with polynomial `0xedb88320`, whose check value for `123456789` is
      `0xcbf43926`. As a type it is an `Algorithm`, so `digest[Crc32]` checksums any digestible
      value through gastronomy (behind `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`). As an
      object it offers the one-shot `.checksum` over several segments and the running
      `.Accumulator`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Crc32.checksum(t"1234".in[Data], t"56789".in[Data])   // 0xcbf43926, as an Int

      val crc = Crc32()
      crc.append(t"123456789".in[Data])
      crc.value                                             // 0xcbf43926L
      ```

      `.checksum` returns the CRC as a signed `Int`, the bits the formats write;
      `Accumulator#value` returns it as an unsigned `Long`.
  refer corpuscular.Crc64
  refer corpuscular.Adler32
  entry .table   the 256-entry lookup table of the bytewise CRC-32 algorithm
      A frozen `Array[Int]`, computed once, indexed by the low byte of the running CRC xor the
      next input byte.
  entry .checksum   the CRC-32 of a sequence of byte segments, taken as one stream, as an `Int`
      As PNG chunks and ZIP entries need: `Crc32.checksum(a, b)` equals the checksum of `a`
      followed by `b`. With no segments, or an empty one, the result is 0.
  entry .apply   a fresh running CRC-32, a `Crc32.Accumulator`
      Returns an exclusive `Accumulator^`; each checksum needs its own.
  entry .Accumulator   the running state of a CRC-32, fed incrementally, as a `Digestion`
    detail
        A mutable `Digestion` whose `append`s, of a whole `Data` or of a window of a mutable
        array, both run the native loop over the array in place, with no copy. `#update` is the
        same loop over a raw `scala.Array[Byte]`, for code which already holds one. `#value`
        reads the CRC as an unsigned number and `#digest` as four big-endian bytes; either may
        be read at any point, and appending may continue afterwards.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val crc = Crc32()
        crc.append(t"1234".in[Data])
        crc.append(t"56789".in[Data])
        crc.value   // 0xcbf43926L
        crc.reset()
        crc.value   // 0L
        ```
    entry #update   feeds `length` bytes of a raw `scala.Array[Byte]` from `index`
        The primitive loop the `append` methods delegate to; it reads the array in place.
    entry #reset    returns the accumulator to its initial state, the CRC of no bytes
    entry #value    the CRC-32 so far, as an unsigned `Long` from 0 to 0xffffffff
    entry #digest   the CRC-32 so far, as four big-endian bytes
  entry #Bits   the width of the checksum: `32`

entry .Crc64   the CRC-64 checksum in the ECMA-182 form XZ uses
  detail
      The reflected CRC-64 with polynomial `0xc96c5795d7870f42`, whose check value for
      `123456789` is `0x995dc9bbdf1939fa`. As a type it is an `Algorithm`, so
      `digest[Crc64]` checksums any digestible value — but only under
      `providers.soundnessProvider`, since the JDK has no CRC-64 and
      `providers.javaBaseProvider` does not offer one. As an object it makes the running
      `.Accumulator`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val crc = Crc64()
      crc.append(t"123456789".in[Data])
      crc.value   // 0x995dc9bbdf1939faL
      ```

      The accumulator uses slicing-by-eight, consuming eight bytes per step through eight
      independent table lookups, which is three to four times faster than the bytewise loop
      and as fast as xz-java's implementation.
  refer corpuscular.Crc32
  entry .table   the eight 256-entry slicing tables of CRC-64, flattened into one array
      A frozen `Array[Long]` of 2048 entries: `table(n)` for `n` under 256 is the classic
      bytewise table, and `table(k*256 + n)` folds a byte `k` positions further into an
      eight-byte word.
  entry .apply   a fresh running CRC-64, a `Crc64.Accumulator`
      Returns an exclusive `Accumulator^`; each checksum needs its own.
  entry .Accumulator   the running state of a CRC-64, fed incrementally, as a `Digestion`
    detail
        A mutable `Digestion` whose `append`s read their input in place, eight bytes at a
        time with a bytewise tail, so a segmented input gives the same checksum as the whole
        at every alignment. `#value` reads the CRC as a `Long` and `#digest` as eight
        big-endian bytes; either may be read at any point, and appending may continue.
    entry #update   feeds `length` bytes of a raw `scala.Array[Byte]` from `index`
        The primitive loop the `append` methods delegate to; it reads the array in place.
    entry #reset    returns the accumulator to its initial state, the CRC of no bytes
    entry #value    the CRC-64 so far, as a `Long`: 0 for no bytes
    entry #digest   the CRC-64 so far, as eight big-endian bytes
  entry #Bits   the width of the checksum: `64`

entry .Adler32   the Adler-32 checksum, as used by the zlib wrapper
  detail
      Adler-32: two running sums modulo 65521, the largest prime below 65536, combined as
      `s2 << 16 | s1`. Its check value for `123456789` is `0x091e01de`, and the checksum of no
      bytes is 1. As a type it is an `Algorithm`, so `digest[Adler32]` checksums any
      digestible value through gastronomy (behind
      `cryptoPermits.permitNonCryptographicHashes`). As an object it makes the running
      `.Accumulator`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val adler = Adler32()
      adler.append(t"123456789".in[Data])
      adler.value   // 0x091e01deL
      ```
  refer corpuscular.Crc32
  entry .apply   a fresh running Adler-32, an `Adler32.Accumulator`
      Returns an exclusive `Accumulator^`; each checksum needs its own.
  entry .Accumulator   the running state of an Adler-32, fed incrementally, as a `Digestion`
    detail
        A mutable `Digestion` whose `append`s read their input in place. `#value` reads the
        checksum as a `Long` and `#digest` as four big-endian bytes; either may be read at any
        point, and appending may continue.
    entry #update   feeds `length` bytes of a raw `scala.Array[Byte]` from `index`
        The primitive loop the `append` methods delegate to; it reads the array in place.
    entry #reset    returns the accumulator to its initial state, whose value is 1
    entry #value    the Adler-32 so far, as a `Long`: 1 for no bytes
    entry #digest   the Adler-32 so far, as four big-endian bytes
  entry #Bits   the width of the checksum: `32`
